# Authored synthesis. Citations resolve to locally preserved evidence records.
SUMMARIES=[
('a130-1985','1985 · A common management framework', '''The first A-130 was dated and effective December 12, 1985, and published on December 24 at 50 FR 52730–52751. The original Federal Register notice is decisive for those dates. Its purpose was broader than computer security: it assembled a government-wide framework for information policy, information technology, privacy, and records management. It superseded A-71, A-90, A-108, and A-121 and carried forward security responsibilities from A-71’s 1978 transmittal. The March 1985 draft was a proposal, not a separate final circular.|The principal organizing authority was the Paperwork Reduction Act of 1980. That Act assigned OMB central information-management responsibilities and required agency information-resource management. The Privacy Act supplied separate obligations for records about individuals; the Brooks Act supplied an earlier statutory framework for automatic data processing and standards. A-130 brought these duties into one management instrument rather than creating their statutory authority. Appendix III established security responsibilities for federal automated information systems, including agency security programs, risk analysis, reviews, and contingency planning. These requirements should be read in their centralized-computing context.|NBS, subsequently renamed NIST, already had a substantial technical program. FIPS 31 addressed physical security, FIPS 46 specified DES, and FIPS 65 addressed risk analysis before A-130 existed. They therefore cannot have originated in response to the 1985 circular. The circular’s reliance on standards and technical guidance is better described as incorporation and coordinated implementation. The Brooks Act itself assigned Commerce/NBS standards functions.|The historical significance is the consolidation of policy and accountability, not the invention of federal computer security. It connected protection of information with program management, procurement and information dissemination. The later Computer Security Act was not enacted until January 1988, so it was not a driver of this original issuance. When comparing editions, use the original 1985 appendix rather than reading the substantially revised 1996 Appendix III backward into it.''', [('a130-1985','50 FR 52730–52751, especially purpose, authorities and Appendix III'),('pra1980','44 USC 3504–3506 as enacted'),('brooks','§111(f)'),('privacy','§3, 552a(e)(10)')]),
('a130-1993','1993 · Information access and the life cycle', '''Transmittal 1 took effect June 25, 1993, and appeared in the Federal Register on July 2, at 58 FR 36068. This revision primarily changed information-management policy, definitions, basic assumptions, privacy-related responsibilities, and explanatory material. It was not the major rewrite of the security appendix. The issuance expressly identifies Appendix III as unchanged. This matters because a chronology that lists only 1994, 1996 and 2000 omits a substantive A-130 revision, while a chronology that calls every revision a new security regime overstates the 1993 action.|OMB emphasized planning across the information life cycle, electronic collection and dissemination, records management, consultation with state and local governments, and agencies’ responsibility to disseminate information consistent with their missions. It consolidated provisions from A-3 on government publications and A-114 on audiovisual activities, rescinding those circulars. It also recognized that government information includes information handled “for” the government, an important precursor to later discussion of outsourced and contractor-operated systems, although not a cloud-specific policy.|The notice identifies amendments to the Paperwork Reduction Act, the Computer Security Act, the Computer Matching and Privacy Protection Act of 1988 and its 1990 amendments. The statutory-authorities section also added the Chief Financial Officers Act. Appendix I addressed the privacy and matching framework; the security implications of those laws should not be confused with a replacement of Appendix III. OMB explained that further stages would address IT management and security.|NIST’s work during this period followed its own Computer Security Act assignments as well as agency needs. A direct causal arrow from the 1993 information-dissemination revision to a particular cryptographic standard is not supported by the evidence. The useful relationship is parallel implementation under shared statutory authorities. This transmittal demonstrates that A-130’s security history is embedded in a larger history of public information, privacy, records and information-resource management.''', [('a130-1993','Background; Structure of this Revision; Summary of Revisions; Appendix I'),('csa','§§2–6')]),
('a130-1994-fr','1994 · IT as a strategic resource', '''Transmittal 2 was effective July 15, 1994, and published July 25 at 59 FR 37906. It addressed information systems and information technology management, particularly section 8b. OMB’s later 1996 preamble confirms both dates and describes its objectives: better public services, less burden, lower administrative costs, and use of IT as a strategic resource to improve federal work processes and organization. The preserved 1994 Federal Register issue allows the actual revised text to be read in context.|The revision strengthened the connection between investment choices, agency missions, planning, management accountability, and information-system performance. It belongs to the transition from managing computer equipment toward managing information resources and outcomes. Security planning had to coexist with those investment and life-cycle decisions. The circular was not simply a catalog of technical safeguards, and its change history should not be reduced to the later authorization process.|Its legal setting included the Paperwork Reduction Act as then amended, the Computer Security Act and the wider move toward performance-oriented management, including the Government Performance and Results Act. Chronology places important limits on interpretation: the Paperwork Reduction Act of 1995 and Clinger–Cohen Act of 1996 had not yet been enacted. Although later consolidated editions cite them, they cannot be the enacted statutory cause of the July 1994 revision. The security-specific rewrite of Appendix III came with Transmittal 3 in 1996.|NIST’s FIPS 140-1, approved in January 1994, established requirements for cryptographic modules within the separate standards program. Its appearance in the same year is not proof that Transmittal 2 directed it. The direct authority is the standards and computer-security legislation identified in the FIPS publication. For a policy reader, the 1994 contribution is therefore strongest in governance: it helped connect technical investments to mission responsibility, while technical standards continued to develop through NIST and Commerce’s assigned functions.''', [('a130-1994-fr','59 FR 37906–37928; section 8b'),('a130-1996-preamble','Opening history of Transmittals 1 and 2'),('fips1401','Authority and applicability')]),
('a130-1996','1996 · Risk-based security management', '''Transmittal 3, dated February 8, 1996, was published February 20 at 61 FR 6428. It substantially revised Appendix III for a distributed, interconnected computing environment. The preamble contrasts that environment with the centralized processing assumptions behind the 1985 appendix and A-71’s 1978 security transmittal. The revision emphasized management responsibility, rules of behavior, training, accountability, security plans, contingency planning, reviews, and authorization, supported by technical controls.|The central statutory response was to the Computer Security Act and the Paperwork Reduction Act of 1995. OMB explicitly incorporated and superseded Bulletins 88-16 and 90-08, which had implemented the Computer Security Act’s security-planning requirements. It reduced centralized submission of security plans and distinguished general support systems from major applications. Risk management remained essential, but a formal quantitative risk analysis was no longer required for every system. The preamble’s response to comments is especially useful evidence: removal of a prescribed analytical form did not remove the duty to assess and manage risk.|NIST guidance was both supporting material and an input. OMB expressly discussed the NIST computer-security handbook and aligned its rules-of-behavior discussion with the handbook’s system-specific policies. Later SP 800-18 security-planning guidance expressly supported the A-130 framework. This is a two-way sequence: existing technical work informs OMB policy, and later implementation guidance helps agencies comply with it. A simple law → circular → every NIST document chain would misdescribe the record.|Clinger–Cohen was enacted on February 10, between the circular’s date and Federal Register publication. The preamble says additional IT-management guidance would follow; it does not present this security transmittal as that full implementation. It also discusses statutory exclusions and national-security directives. These are real scope boundaries, distinct from the fact that NIST has its own affirmative statutory duties. Read the 1996 appendix as a major security-management reform, not as the final form of the modern FISMA/RMF system.''', [('a130-1996','Transmittal heading; Appendix III, parts A–B'),('a130-1996-fr','61 FR 6428–6431, preamble'),('csa','§§3–6'),('sp80018','Introduction and authority')]),
('a130-2000','2000 · Investment, architecture and electronic transactions', '''Transmittal 4 is dated November 28, 2000; its final notice was published December 12 at 65 FR 77677. The April 13 notice at 65 FR 19933 was the proposed revision and must not be cited as the final issuance. The main purpose was further implementation of Clinger–Cohen, together with related laws and Executive Order 13011. The final notice documents the comments OMB received and the decisions it made before issuing the revision.|The circular strengthened capital planning and investment control, enterprise architecture, agency CIO responsibilities, and the connection between IT investments and mission performance. It integrated security into investment decisions rather than treating protection as an expense added after system design. The final notice expressly identifies incorporation of M-00-07, on funding security in information-system investments, into section 8b(3). This is a documented memorandum → circular relationship. Earlier implementation memoranda also supplied parts of the capital-planning and architecture framework.|A second important track was the Government Paperwork Elimination Act of 1998. OMB’s April 25, 2000 guidance, published May 2, was incorporated as Appendix II. It addressed electronic records and signatures, including risk-sensitive choices about electronic transactions. The separate guidance and its subsequent incorporation should appear as distinct events; neither is a wholly new A-130 security appendix. The downloaded transmittal PDF contains the main circular and lists the appendices; the separately preserved appendix texts supply their content.|GISRA was enacted shortly before this transmittal and led to further security-program and reporting instructions. Temporal proximity alone does not establish GISRA as the purpose of this Clinger–Cohen revision. FISMA 2002 came later still. Subsequent NIST categorization, minimum-security and control publications were developed under FISMA’s direct statutory assignments while operating alongside the 2000 circular. This edition illustrates A-130’s role as an integrating management instrument whose core text can remain in place while laws, memoranda and technical standards continue to change.''', [('a130-2000','Sections 3, 8b, 9'),('a130-2000-notice','Background; comments; incorporation of M-00-07 and M-00-10'),('clinger','Division E'),('gisra','Title X, Subtitle G')]),
('a130-2016','2016 · Security and privacy throughout the life cycle', '''The 2016 rewrite, effective July 28 and announced in the Federal Register that day at 81 FR 49689, retitled A-130 “Managing Information as a Strategic Resource.” It replaced the 2000 edition after sixteen years of substantial legal and technical change. Its purpose was to integrate information management, IT governance, security, privacy, acquisition and workforce responsibilities across the information life cycle. Unlike an incremental transmittal, it reorganized the circular and its appendices.|The authority section lists the statutory framework, including the Paperwork Reduction Act, Clinger–Cohen, the E-Government Act, FISMA and other information-management authorities. FISMA 2014’s division of responsibility among OMB, DHS, agencies and NIST is central to the security provisions. Appendix I addresses protection and management of federal information resources; Appendix II addresses personally identifiable information and the privacy program. The older Appendix III numbering must not be carried forward as though the structure were unchanged.|The circular connects agency risk management, authorization, ongoing assessment and monitoring with NIST standards and guidelines. It requires security and privacy considerations in planning and acquisition and addresses information handled on agencies’ behalf. It also expressly mentions cloud-based services. Its government-wide responsibilities explain Commerce/NIST functions with statutory citations; those functions are not created solely by A-130. NIST’s FIPS 199, FIPS 200 and earlier RMF and control publications predate this rewrite, making later incorporation the appropriate relationship for much of the technical material.|The change is substantial for privacy governance: privacy risk and controls are treated across the life cycle and alongside security, rather than as a synonym for confidentiality. Subsequent SP 800-37 Revision 2 reflects the integrated security/privacy approach. Later cloud, identity, zero-trust, software and post-quantum memoranda extend particular domains without themselves constituting comprehensive revisions of A-130. This search located no later comprehensive circular revision. That finding is bounded by the official collections searched through October 10, 2026; it does not mean every provision remains unaffected by later law or policy.''', [('a130-2016','Sections 3, 5, 6, 9; Appendices I–II'),('a130-2016-notice','81 FR 49689'),('fisma2014','44 USC 3553–3554, 3557'),('sp800372','Authority; §§1–2')])]

SYNTHESIS='''A-130 is a central operating framework for federal information management, but it is not the sole source of federal information-security obligations. Congress assigned responsibilities directly to OMB, agency heads, NIST and the Secretary of Commerce. OMB uses the circular and memoranda to organize agency implementation; NIST develops standards and guidance under statutory and executive assignments; Commerce promulgates applicable standards; agencies implement and accept risk within their authority. These streams intersect repeatedly.|The strongest A-130 contribution is management integration: accountable officials, life-cycle planning, security and privacy programs, investment decisions, authorization and ongoing risk management. The strongest independent NIST lineage is technical standards work, particularly cryptography and FISMA’s categorization/minimum-security program. Identity, controlled unclassified information and software security also have distinct presidential, statutory or regulatory drivers. “Independent statutory duty” does not mean “OMB has no oversight authority.”|For cloud services, the path combines general A-130/FISMA obligations with the 2011 FedRAMP memorandum, NIST cloud guidance, the 2022 FedRAMP Authorization Act and M-24-15 in 2024. Commercial outsourcing existed in the policy record long before cloud terminology. A hit for commercial sources or contractor-operated systems is therefore displayed separately from an exact match for cloud, commercial services or FedRAMP.'''

PREHISTORY='''The administrative lineage begins before A-130. Bureau of the Budget Circular A-71, dated March 6, 1965, allocated responsibilities for automatic data processing among agencies and central organizations. A government-hosted copy of FIPS 12 reproduces that circular in §4.6. The Brooks Act followed on October 30, 1965; its §111(f) assigned Commerce a standards and technical-services role. Because A-71 predates that enactment, the original A-71 cannot be described as a response to the enacted Brooks Act. Earlier ADP acquisition and sharing policies, including A-54 and A-27 referenced in the reproduction, supply administrative context rather than a modern cybersecurity regime.|The Privacy Act of 1974 imposed safeguards for records about individuals and assigned OMB guidance responsibilities. NBS security publications such as FIPS 31, FIPS 46 and FIPS 65 already existed before 1985. A-71’s July 27, 1978 Transmittal Memorandum 1 addressed security of automated information systems. The 1996 A-130 preamble expressly identifies it as the predecessor of the 1985 security appendix. A standalone original of that transmittal was not recovered in this search; the report uses the later OMB preamble and contemporaneous NBS SP 500-85 as evidence of its role, not as substitutes labeled “original A-71.”|The Paperwork Reduction Act of 1980 broadened the information-resources framework. A-130 consolidated it in 1985. After enactment of the Computer Security Act on January 8, 1988, OMB Bulletins 88-16 (July 6, 1988) and 90-08 (July 9, 1990) supplied security-plan instructions. The 1996 preamble documents both their titles and their incorporation and supersession. Their standalone originals remain a recovery gap. The distinction is consequential: an OMB statement that it superseded a bulletin is strong evidence of the relationship, but not evidence that this collection holds every word of the original bulletin.'''

DOMAINS=[
('Agency governance and life-cycle management','A-130 is a principal implementation instrument','PRA agency/OMB duties and FISMA security-program duties supply the legal base. A-130 links them to planning, CIO responsibilities, investment, risk and accountability. NIST supports implementation rather than supplying all agency governance authority.','a130-2016','§5 and Appendix I; PRA 1995 §§3504–3506; FISMA 2014 §§3553–3554'),
('Categorization and minimum security','Direct law → NIST/Commerce, then OMB/agency integration','FISMA 2002 §303 assigned NIST development of risk categories, guidelines and minimum requirements. FIPS 199 and 200 explicitly identify that mandate. FIPS 200 requires selecting and implementing appropriate controls using SP 800-53. The 2016 circular integrates an already established technical framework.','fisma2002','Title III §§303–304; FIPS 199 foreword; FIPS 200 §§1–4'),
('Risk management and authorization','Shared implementation, with strong A-130 management direction','The 1996 appendix required management authorization and reviews. SP 800-37 developed certification/accreditation guidance in 2004, the RMF in 2010 and integrated security/privacy in 2018. NIST methodology and OMB accountability are complementary; a technical publication alone does not transfer the authorizing official’s responsibility.','sp800372','Authority; §2; Appendix D; A-130 2016 Appendix I'),
('Cryptography','Strong independent technical-standard lineage','DES predates A-130. The Computer Security Act and later standards statutes support Commerce/NIST work; FIPS 140 and 197 identify their authorities. The 2022 quantum statute assigns OMB migration duties keyed to NIST standards. FIPS 203–205 and the 2026 migration memo illustrate standards development and deployment policy as distinct tasks.','quantumact','§4; FIPS 203–205 forewords; M-26-15 overview'),
('Identity and authentication','Separate presidential and OMB tasking also matter','HSPD-12 directs a common identification standard, leading to FIPS 201. M-04-04 expressly calls for NIST electronic-authentication guidance, leading to SP 800-63. M-19-17 later rescinds M-04-04 and updates identity policy. Neither lineage should be attributed solely to an A-130 revision.','hspd12','¶¶2–4; M-04-04; SP 800-63 v1.0.1 §1; M-19-17 p. 13'),
('Contractor systems and CUI','Scope and contractual adoption matter','A-130 applies to federal information handled on behalf of agencies. SP 800-171 concerns CUI in nonfederal systems and has an EO 13556/CUI-program lineage. Its use in a contract must be traced to the applicable rule or contract; publication of a NIST guideline alone does not bind every commercial provider.','sp8001713','§1, purpose and applicability; EO 13556 §§2–5'),
('Cloud and FedRAMP','A-130 baseline plus program-specific law and policy','The 2011 unnumbered memo established the reusable cloud authorization approach. The 2022 Act codified FedRAMP responsibilities. M-24-15 implements and modernizes the program and rescinds the 2011 memo. NIST definitions and cloud guidance support the program but do not alone establish its statutory requirements.','m-24-15','Opening; scope; agency responsibilities; concluding rescission'),
('National-security systems','An actual limitation, not a NIST takeover','FISMA and A-130 contain scope exclusions and special treatment for national-security systems. The 2016 circular §3 says agencies are not required to apply its policies to those systems and must follow applicable statutes, executive orders and presidential directives. This separate security regime is not a grant of general leadership to NIST over it.','a130-2016','§3; FISMA 2014 44 USC 3557')]

NIST_GROUPS=[
('Before A-130: physical security, risk and continuity','FIPS 31, FIPS 65 and NBS SP 500-85 show a technical-guidance infrastructure predating the circular. SP 500-85 explicitly helps managers comply with A-71’s contingency-planning responsibilities. This is documented implementation support for an earlier OMB instrument, while DES illustrates standards authority already in operation. Some older scans extract without reliable word spacing; an absent text-search hit is not a verified absence.', [('fips31','Scope'),('fips65','Introduction'),('sp50085','Abstract and question 6'),('fips46','Authority')]),
('Security plans and the 1996 management model','SP 800-12 supplies a broad management and technical handbook; the 1996 preamble explicitly discusses it. SP 800-18 (1998, revised 2006) turns security planning into practical guidance. SP 800-30 (2002, revised 2012) supports risk assessment. The direction differs across these documents: the handbook informed OMB’s explanation, while the planning guide expressly supports the circular’s requirements. The archive holds historical editions so those relationships can be examined without silently substituting today’s text.', [('a130-1996-preamble','Responses 5–6'),('sp80012','Introduction'),('sp80018','Introduction'),('sp800181','§1'),('sp80030','§1'),('sp800301','§1')]),
('FISMA’s categorization → minimum requirements → controls chain','FIPS 199 (2004) defines impact categorization; FIPS 200 (2006) establishes minimum requirements and connects selection of controls to SP 800-53. The original SP 800-53 (2005) and later revisions evolve the control catalog. Revision 4 (2013) includes a privacy-control appendix. Revision 5 (2020) integrates security and privacy controls and separates baseline selection into SP 800-53B. This collection preserves selected major editions, not every erratum, release or intermediate revision. The statute is a direct driver; A-130 incorporates and organizes agency use of the resulting materials.', [('fisma2002','§303'),('fips199','Foreword; §3'),('fips200','§§3–4'),('sp80053','§1'),('sp800534','Appendix J'),('sp800535','§1'),('sp80053b','§2')]),
('Authorization becomes a continuous risk-management process','SP 800-37 (2004) described certification and accreditation; Revision 1 (2010) framed a life-cycle RMF; Revision 2 (2018) added organizational preparation and integrated privacy responsibilities. SP 800-137 (2011) supports continuous monitoring. The circular and FISMA establish management duties; these publications develop a method for carrying them out. Continuous monitoring should not be mistaken for absence of authorization or automatic acceptance of every risk.', [('sp80037','§1'),('sp800371','§2'),('sp800372','§§1–2'),('sp800137','§1')]),
('Cryptographic standards have their own lineage','FIPS 46 specified DES in 1977, before A-130. FIPS 140-1, 140-2 and 140-3 govern cryptographic-module security; FIPS 197 specifies AES. Their authority statements concern the federal standards program, not a claim that one circular ordered each algorithm. FIPS 203, 204 and 205 (2024) standardize post-quantum mechanisms. The Quantum Computing Cybersecurity Preparedness Act links federal migration policy to NIST standards, and M-26-15 (June 24, 2026) implements accelerated migration while citing A-130’s life-cycle security duty. Historical algorithm publication, later withdrawal and module-validation transition are distinct events; this report does not recommend historical algorithms for use.', [('fips46','Authority'),('fips1401','Authority'),('fips1402','Authority'),('fips1403','Foreword'),('fips197','Authority'),('fips203','Foreword'),('fips204','Foreword'),('fips205','Foreword'),('m-26-15','Overview and system modernization')]),
('Identity: a clear example of explicit direction','HSPD-12, issued August 27, 2004, directed Commerce to develop a common identification standard; FIPS 201 followed in 2005. The original FIPS 201 file was not recovered at the attempted archive URLs, so it is marked unavailable; FIPS 201-3 is preserved. Separately, M-04-04 directed NIST to develop electronic-authentication technical guidance. The collection holds SP 800-63 version 1.0.1 (September 2004), not a mislabeled June first edition, and the 2025 SP 800-63-4. M-19-17 documents rescission of older identity memoranda. These chains demonstrate why “all NIST publications flow from A-130” is too broad.', [('hspd12','¶¶2–4'),('fips2013','Foreword'),('m-04-04','NIST technical guidance assignment'),('sp80063v101','§1'),('sp800634','Introduction'),('m-19-17','p. 13')]),
('Cloud, nonfederal systems, zero trust and software','SP 800-145 defines cloud computing; SP 800-144 analyzes public-cloud security and privacy. Neither publication itself establishes FedRAMP. SP 800-171 implements the technical protection side of the CUI framework; applicability depends on the relevant CUI rules and agreements. SP 800-207 (2020) predates EO 14028 and M-22-09, which later use zero-trust concepts for agency modernization. SP 800-218’s secure-development framework is part of the software-security response to EO 14028, with M-22-18 and M-23-16 supplying acquisition implementation. These are documented independent drivers and later incorporation, not merely dates near a circular revision.', [('sp800145','§2'),('sp800144','Executive summary'),('sp8001713','§1'),('eo13556','§§2–5'),('sp800207','§1'),('eo14028','§§3–4'),('m-22-09','Strategy'),('sp800218','§1'),('m-22-18','Agency requirements'),('m-23-16','Updated guidance')])]
